# Jar, Tin and Pot have a supertype in common and no constructor in common: none calls another. Jar and Tin meet at a parameter of their own: "the constructors of the family", a type that does not say who came. That value comes, by a longer way, to a parameter that holds a plain function already, and is constructed there with a text.
# All constructors of the class family are one family of functions with `plain`. If they were lost one by one and not united, the text would come to Jar alone, whose name the lost value bears, and not to Tin. Pot came nowhere, and is handed the text all the same: nothing may hang on who came first.
# function either(a) { return a; }  function make(held, x) { return new held(x); }  make(plain, "text");  make(pass(pass(either(Jar))), "text");  either(Tin);
